          "title": "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "9e0c6876-b1a4-5102-a706-2d6c5f85581d",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}=5.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,-1).$\n\nUse $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}.$ The nonzero gradient gives the direction of the normal line. A line through $(x_0,y_0)$ parallel to $\\left\\langle a,b\\right\\rangle$ satisfies $b(x-x_0)=a(y-y_0).$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=2 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=5.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=4 x \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=6 y \\\\\n\\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\left\\langle 4,-6\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 4,-6\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(-6\\right)\\left(x - 1\\right)&=\\left(4\\right)\\left(y + 1\\right) \\\\\n- 6 x - 4 y + 2&=0 \\\\\n3 x + 2 y - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $3 x + 2 y - 1=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead.",
