          ":question/id": "d7c5eacf-d850-58ea-879b-3e91e7d62722",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $2 x + y - 1=2 x + y + \\sqrt{- y^{2} + \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}} - 4.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-1,0).$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\sqrt{- y^{2} + \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=3.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{x - 2}{\\sqrt{- y^{2} + \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}}} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{- y^{2} + \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}}} \\\\\n\\nabla f(-1,0)&=\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(0\\right)\\left(x + 1\\right)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\left(y\\right) \\\\\ny&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $y=0.$ This is a horizontal line. The direction relation avoids division by a zero gradient component.",
